description:
In pack_exec.rs prepare() (~line 631), delta_deps_hot gets an explicit reserve before its push loop, but external_base_oids only gets .clear() with no reserve. Every .push(oid) in the subsequent loop may reallocate if the count of external bases exceeds the prior high-water mark. This violates the zero-allocation-after-startup contract.

Fix: Count external deps upfront before the loop, then reserve capacity using the same monotonic-growth pattern used for all other scratch fields:

```rust
let external_count = plan.delta_deps.iter()
    .filter(|dep| matches!(dep.base, BaseLoc::External { .. }))
    .count();
if self.external_base_oids.capacity() < external_count {
    self.external_base_oids
        .reserve(external_count - self.external_base_oids.capacity());
}
self.external_base_oids.clear();
```

File: src/git_scan/pack_exec.rs, PackExecScratch::prepare() method.

The safety of the get_unchecked call in the work queue loop (src/engine/core.rs:1433) depends on the invariant that work_q is append-only during the loop. Currently this is enforced by convention and SAFETY comments, not by the type system.

Consider introducing a view type that wraps &mut ScratchVec<WorkItem> and exposes only read access + push(), making shrink operations (clear/truncate/pop/drain) a compile error inside the loop body.

Example sketch:

  struct WorkQueueView<'a> {
      inner: &'a mut ScratchVec<WorkItem>,
  }

  impl<'a> WorkQueueView<'a> {
      fn len(&self) -> usize { self.inner.len() }
      unsafe fn get_unchecked(&self, index: usize) -> &WorkItem {
          self.inner.get_unchecked(index)
      }
      fn push(&mut self, item: WorkItem) { self.inner.push(item) }
  }

This would turn the append-only invariant from a comment into a type-system guarantee. The scan loop (core.rs:1411-1460) would construct the view before entering the while loop and drop it after.

Low priority — the current approach is safe (debug_assert catches violations, Kani+Miri+proptest verify), and work_q is pub(super) scoped so mutations can only come from within the engine module. Revisit if more get_unchecked regression guards accumulate.

Stop storing a Layout per ScratchVec instance; compute it on drop instead.

## Problem
ScratchVec<T> stores (ptr, len, cap, layout) = 40 bytes per instance. The Layout (16 bytes) is only used in Drop to deallocate. ScanScratch has 11 ScratchVec instances, so this wastes 176 bytes of always-present metadata.

## Solution
Remove the layout field and compute it in Drop:
```rust
pub struct ScratchVec<T> {
    ptr: NonNull<MaybeUninit<T>>,
    len: usize,
    cap: usize,
    // layout removed — computed from cap + size_of::<T>() + align in Drop
}

impl<T> Drop for ScratchVec<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        if self.cap > 0 {
            let layout = Self::layout_for(self.cap);
            unsafe { std::alloc::dealloc(self.ptr.as_ptr().cast(), layout); }
        }
    }
}

fn layout_for(cap: usize) -> Layout {
    let bytes = cap.checked_mul(std::mem::size_of::<T>()).unwrap();
    let align = PAGE_SIZE_MIN.max(std::mem::align_of::<T>());
    Layout::from_size_align(bytes, align).unwrap()
}
```

## Impact
- Shrinks each ScratchVec header by 16 bytes (40→24 bytes)
- 11 instances in ScanScratch: saves 176 bytes of hot metadata
- Drop is cold path — slight extra instructions there are fine

## Files to modify
- src/scratch_memory.rs — remove layout field, compute in drop/grow

## Correctness
- Add size guard assertion: ScratchVec<u8> header ≤ 24 bytes
- Ensure layout_for() produces identical layout to what was stored
